WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.270
Welcome to Module 7, Final Dataset in
Stata. In Module 6, you

00:00:04.270 --> 00:00:05.040
cleaned and

00:00:05.040 --> 00:00:08.701
standardized each GGIR output. Now you'll
bring those pieces

00:00:08.701 --> 00:00:09.860
together, appending

00:00:09.860 --> 00:00:13.043
batches, merging person-, day-, and
night-level files,

00:00:13.043 --> 00:00:14.340
deriving your analysis

00:00:14.340 --> 00:00:18.184
variables, and applying reliability and
exclusion rules to

00:00:18.184 --> 00:00:18.780
build one

00:00:18.780 --> 00:00:23.643
reproducible analysis-ready dataset.
Module 8 then checks what

00:00:23.643 --> 00:00:24.820
you've learned.

00:00:25.160 --> 00:00:29.454
The whole module is one 11-step Stata
plan. You set up by

00:00:29.454 --> 00:00:30.960
importing the Part 5

00:00:30.960 --> 00:00:34.343
files and cleaning IDs. You combine them,
append the

00:00:34.343 --> 00:00:36.100
collection lots, then merge

00:00:36.100 --> 00:00:39.744
the day-level and quality files, and clean
the sleep records.

00:00:39.744 --> 00:00:40.640
You derive MVPA

00:00:40.640 --> 00:00:45.379
and inactivity bouts, and count valid
days. And you finalize.

00:00:45.379 --> 00:00:46.000
Evaluate

00:00:46.000 --> 00:00:49.055
reliability, collapse to
participant-level, apply the

00:00:49.055 --> 00:00:49.920
three-parameter

00:00:49.920 --> 00:00:54.242
exclusions, and save a dated .dta. Keep
this map in mind.

00:00:54.242 --> 00:00:55.380
Every clip that

00:00:55.380 --> 00:00:59.753
follows is one of these steps. Two
operations do most of the

00:00:59.753 --> 00:01:00.700
work and it's

00:01:00.700 --> 00:01:04.224
vital not to confuse them. Append stacks
files on top of each

00:01:04.224 --> 00:01:05.380
other. It adds rows.

00:01:05.380 --> 00:01:09.056
Use it to combine collection lots into one
file and keep a

00:01:09.056 --> 00:01:10.260
source batch marker

00:01:10.260 --> 00:01:13.935
so you never lose track of where a row
came from. Merge joins

00:01:13.935 --> 00:01:15.020
files side-by-side

00:01:15.280 --> 00:01:19.188
by a key. It adds columns. A one-to-one
merge links day rows

00:01:19.188 --> 00:01:20.360
by participant and

00:01:20.360 --> 00:01:24.133
date. A many-to-one merge attaches one
participant-level

00:01:24.133 --> 00:01:25.480
record to every day.

00:01:25.480 --> 00:01:30.623
The key is what protects your unit of
observation. Before we

00:01:30.623 --> 00:01:32.080
combine anything,

00:01:32.080 --> 00:01:36.639
let's confirm what one row means. The
person quality file has

00:01:36.639 --> 00:01:37.760
one row per ID,

00:01:37.760 --> 00:01:42.432
nine people. The day file has one row per
participant-day, 31

00:01:42.432 --> 00:01:44.040
rows, and isid proves

00:01:44.100 --> 00:01:49.278
that's unique. We load the night file the
same way. Now we

00:01:49.278 --> 00:01:51.420
append the lots. Batch A

00:01:51.420 --> 00:01:54.867
and Batch B stack into one day file and a
source batch flag

00:01:54.867 --> 00:01:55.860
records which lot

00:01:55.860 --> 00:02:01.243
each row came from, 18 from A, 13 from B.
isid confirms one

00:02:01.243 --> 00:02:02.520
unique row per

00:02:02.520 --> 00:02:08.379
participant-day. Next, a one-to-one merge,
the day file to the

00:02:08.379 --> 00:02:10.080
cleaned sleep file

00:02:10.180 --> 00:02:14.784
on participant and date. We preserve,
merge, and inspect

00:02:14.784 --> 00:02:16.100
_merge, how many

00:02:16.100 --> 00:02:20.244
matched, and which came from one side.
Restore brings us back.

00:02:20.244 --> 00:02:21.180
Always check a

00:02:21.180 --> 00:02:26.896
merge. Now a many-to-one merge and the
direction matters. In

00:02:26.896 --> 00:02:28.420
the person-level

00:02:28.420 --> 00:02:32.559
quality file, each participant appears
once. In our working

00:02:32.559 --> 00:02:33.260
data, each

00:02:33.260 --> 00:02:37.700
participant has many day rows, so we merge
many-to-one on

00:02:37.700 --> 00:02:39.180
participant ID. One

00:02:39.180 --> 00:02:42.529
quality record is broadcast onto every day
for that person.

00:02:42.529 --> 00:02:43.380
Notice the keep

00:02:43.380 --> 00:02:46.664
using list. We pull in only the variables
we need, the

00:02:46.664 --> 00:02:47.820
clipping score, the

00:02:47.820 --> 00:02:52.172
calibration error, the complete_24hcycle
completeness score,

00:02:52.172 --> 00:02:52.680
and the

00:02:52.680 --> 00:02:56.553
individual quality flags, including
qc_three_parameter_pass.

00:02:56.553 --> 00:02:57.780
After the merge, we

00:02:57.780 --> 00:03:02.960
always tabulate _merge. A value of three
means the day

00:03:02.960 --> 00:03:06.960
matched a participant record. A one would
mean a day with no

00:03:06.960 --> 00:03:08.160
quality data and a

00:03:08.160 --> 00:03:12.829
two, a participant with no days. Here,
everything matches.

00:03:12.829 --> 00:03:14.520
Every day now carries

00:03:14.520 --> 00:03:19.078
the participant's quality information,
ready for filtering.

00:03:19.078 --> 00:03:20.160
With the files

00:03:20.160 --> 00:03:23.446
combined, we derive the numbers our rules
will need, but we

00:03:23.446 --> 00:03:24.560
build them before we

00:03:24.560 --> 00:03:28.610
apply any cutoff. We count valid days per
participant, flag

00:03:28.610 --> 00:03:29.640
weekdays versus

00:03:29.640 --> 00:03:32.697
weekends so we can require weekend
coverage, and keep days

00:03:32.697 --> 00:03:33.540
with at least 10

00:03:33.540 --> 00:03:36.832
hours of awake wear. Deriving first keeps
the cutoffs

00:03:36.832 --> 00:03:38.260
transparent and easy to

00:03:38.260 --> 00:03:42.729
change. Now we build the day count
variables the exclusion

00:03:42.729 --> 00:03:43.500
rules will

00:03:43.500 --> 00:03:47.889
depend on. First, we flag weekend days. We
generate a weekend

00:03:47.889 --> 00:03:49.400
indicator, set to one

00:03:49.400 --> 00:03:53.107
when the day type is Saturday or Sunday.
Then run bysort

00:03:53.107 --> 00:03:54.100
participant_id:

00:03:54.100 --> 00:03:57.336
generate byte n_days_from_daily = _N, the
number of cleaned

00:03:57.336 --> 00:03:59.420
day rows each participant contributes.

00:03:59.420 --> 00:04:03.066
day rows each participant contributes.
Because bysort groups

00:04:03.066 --> 00:04:04.220
the data by person,

00:04:04.220 --> 00:04:07.997
every row for that participant receives
the same total. Next,

00:04:07.997 --> 00:04:08.740
still within

00:04:08.740 --> 00:04:11.713
participant, use egen with total() to sum
the weekend

00:04:11.713 --> 00:04:13.900
indicator, giving n_weekend_from_daily,

00:04:13.900 --> 00:04:17.172
from daily, the count of valid weekend
days per person.

00:04:17.172 --> 00:04:18.660
Finally, we tabulate both

00:04:18.660 --> 00:04:22.169
so we can see the distribution, how many
valid days people

00:04:22.169 --> 00:04:23.500
have, and whether each

00:04:23.500 --> 00:04:27.370
has weekend coverage. Notice that nothing
has been excluded

00:04:27.370 --> 00:04:28.420
yet. We are only

00:04:28.420 --> 00:04:31.534
measuring and deriving clean, transparent
counts first, so

00:04:31.534 --> 00:04:32.500
that when we apply

00:04:32.500 --> 00:04:36.428
cutoffs later, every decision is visible
and easy to justify.

00:04:36.428 --> 00:04:37.780
These per participant

00:04:37.780 --> 00:04:40.886
counts are exactly what the reliability
estimate and the

00:04:40.886 --> 00:04:41.940
exclusion rule will

00:04:41.940 --> 00:04:47.368
later read. Before collapsing, verify. We
derive day counts

00:04:47.368 --> 00:04:49.300
ourselves, but GGIR's

00:04:49.300 --> 00:04:53.562
Part 5 already reports a count too. Trust,
then verify. Match

00:04:53.562 --> 00:04:54.820
your derived count

00:04:54.820 --> 00:04:58.315
against Part 5, assert that every record
merged, and check

00:04:58.315 --> 00:04:59.460
that the two counts

00:04:59.460 --> 00:05:04.420
disagree for nobody. Only reconciled data
should move forward.

00:05:04.420 --> 00:05:05.700
Now we reconcile

00:05:05.700 --> 00:05:09.613
against GGIR's own numbers. We merge the
participant-level

00:05:09.613 --> 00:05:11.300
Part 5 summary one-to-one

00:05:11.300 --> 00:05:15.604
on participant_id. All five participants
match, and assert

00:05:15.604 --> 00:05:16.420
_merge == 3

00:05:16.420 --> 00:05:20.643
turns that into a hard rule. If any record
failed to match,

00:05:20.643 --> 00:05:21.860
Stata would stop.

00:05:21.860 --> 00:05:25.384
Correction: One participant's day count
disagrees; weekend

00:05:25.384 --> 00:05:26.660
counts agree for all.

00:05:26.660 --> 00:05:31.940
Stop and reconcile the day-count
difference before proceeding.

00:05:31.940 --> 00:05:37.220
Reconciled data—and only reconciled
data—move on.

00:05:39.220 --> 00:05:43.113
Now we change the grain. Collapsing takes
many day-level rows

00:05:43.113 --> 00:05:44.900
per participant and produces

00:05:44.900 --> 00:05:49.503
one analysis-ready row each using means
and counts. Clean day

00:05:49.503 --> 00:05:51.540
count, clean weekend count,

00:05:51.540 --> 00:05:55.050
and average bout measures all become
participant-level

00:05:55.050 --> 00:05:55.700
variables.

00:05:55.700 --> 00:05:58.340
This is the shape your analysis will
actually use.

00:06:00.420 --> 00:06:04.281
Here we inspect the clean
participant-level counts. First, we

00:06:04.281 --> 00:06:06.180
format the two count variables

00:06:06.180 --> 00:06:09.550
so they display as whole numbers. A quick
count confirms five

00:06:09.550 --> 00:06:10.820
participants and assert

00:06:10.820 --> 00:06:15.229
r(N) == 5 makes that explicit. If the
number ever changed, the

00:06:15.229 --> 00:06:16.580
do-file would halt.

00:06:16.580 --> 00:06:20.631
isid participant_id confirms the data is
now one row per

00:06:20.631 --> 00:06:23.380
person. We've truly left the day-level

00:06:23.380 --> 00:06:27.528
behind. Then we tabulate clean_day_count.
Four participants

00:06:27.528 --> 00:06:30.340
have six cleaned days and one has seven.

00:06:30.340 --> 00:06:34.190
Every person clears the four-day minimum
we'll require. Next,

00:06:34.190 --> 00:06:36.020
tabulate clean_weekend_count.

00:06:36.740 --> 00:06:40.346
All five have at least one valid weekend
day, so weekend

00:06:40.346 --> 00:06:42.020
coverage is satisfied too.

00:06:42.020 --> 00:06:45.608
These simple frequency tables are doing
important work. They

00:06:45.608 --> 00:06:47.940
let you see, before any exclusion runs,

00:06:47.940 --> 00:06:51.317
exactly who would pass or fail each rule.
Never apply a cutoff

00:06:51.317 --> 00:06:53.060
you haven't first looked at in a

00:06:53.060 --> 00:06:56.387
table like this. It's a small habit that
saves you from silent

00:06:56.387 --> 00:06:57.460
mistakes downstream.

00:06:59.460 --> 00:07:03.877
Method note: No 10-hour awake-wear
exclusion was applied in

00:07:03.877 --> 00:07:05.300
this demonstration.

00:07:05.300 --> 00:07:10.981
Treat clean_day_count as a cleaned-day
count, not a valid-day

00:07:10.981 --> 00:07:11.540
count,

00:07:11.540 --> 00:07:17.460
until the study's awake-wear rule is
implemented and verified.

00:07:17.460 --> 00:07:21.167
named and documented. Then summarize
across the key variables.

00:07:21.167 --> 00:07:23.140
The clean day count averages just

00:07:23.140 --> 00:07:27.175
over six with clean weekend count at two.
Awake wear time,

00:07:27.175 --> 00:07:29.540
sleep duration, and the cumulative

00:07:29.540 --> 00:07:34.252
MVPA bout measures. One minute plus, five
minute plus, and ten

00:07:34.252 --> 00:07:37.140
minute plus. All now carry participant

00:07:37.140 --> 00:07:40.743
level means. Look how compact this is
compared to where we

00:07:40.743 --> 00:07:42.980
started. Raw sub-second acceleration

00:07:42.980 --> 00:07:47.218
became GGIR part files, which we appended,
merged, cleaned,

00:07:47.218 --> 00:07:50.020
derived, reconciled, and collapsed into

00:07:50.020 --> 00:07:53.633
this. Every variable here traces back to a
source file and a

00:07:53.633 --> 00:07:55.620
documented decision. Save it with

00:07:55.620 --> 00:07:58.393
today's date in the file name, and this
becomes your

00:07:58.393 --> 00:08:00.420
analysis-ready dataset. From here, the

00:08:00.420 --> 00:08:03.846
modeling is straightforward, because the
careful data work is

00:08:03.846 --> 00:08:05.700
already done and fully traceable.

00:08:07.540 --> 00:08:11.001
One method deserves special mention: the
Spearman–Brown

00:08:11.001 --> 00:08:13.140
prophecy formula. It estimates how

00:08:13.140 --> 00:08:16.402
reliability changes with the number of
measurement days. A

00:08:16.402 --> 00:08:18.820
single day is noisy. More valid days
better

00:08:18.820 --> 00:08:22.889
capture habitual activity, so reliability
rises and then

00:08:22.889 --> 00:08:25.940
plateaus. The idea transfers to any study.

00:08:25.940 --> 00:08:29.346
Estimate reliability at your current
number of days, project

00:08:29.346 --> 00:08:31.220
it for more or fewer, use that to

00:08:31.220 --> 00:08:35.018
justify your minimum valid-day rule and
report it openly. It

00:08:35.018 --> 00:08:36.980
turns a valid-day cutoff into a

00:08:36.980 --> 00:08:41.356
measurement decision instead of an
arbitrary one. For the

00:08:41.356 --> 00:08:44.580
course example, final inclusion uses three

00:08:44.580 --> 00:08:48.495
parameters. At least four valid days, at
least 10 hours of

00:08:48.495 --> 00:08:51.060
awake wear per valid-day, and at least

00:08:51.060 --> 00:08:54.594
one weekend day. Keep an exclusion log
that reports how many

00:08:54.594 --> 00:08:56.420
participants each rule removed,

00:08:56.420 --> 00:09:00.016
how many failed several, and the final
analytic sample. And a

00:09:00.016 --> 00:09:02.020
critical point, define these rules

00:09:02.020 --> 00:09:06.271
from your data management and analysis
plan, never after

00:09:06.271 --> 00:09:09.460
seeing the results. Save your outputs as a

00:09:09.460 --> 00:09:13.537
set, not a single file. A data.dta, a data
dictionary, a

00:09:13.537 --> 00:09:15.940
cleaning log, an exclusion table,

00:09:15.940 --> 00:09:19.826
the Stata do-file, and a readme naming the
source GGIR run and

00:09:19.826 --> 00:09:22.020
batch dates. Stamp the dataset with

00:09:22.020 --> 00:09:24.276
its date. For example,

00:09:24.276 --> 00:09:29.914
accelerometer_final_analysis_yyyymmdd.dta,
so the exact

00:09:29.914 --> 00:09:32.580
version is never in doubt.

00:09:34.820 --> 00:09:37.988
That's module seven. Your final dataset
should be

00:09:37.988 --> 00:09:41.220
reproducible. One do-file rebuilds it from
the raw

00:09:41.220 --> 00:09:45.373
GGIR outputs. Dated, every file carries
its date, and

00:09:45.373 --> 00:09:48.820
explainable. Every variable traces back to
a

00:09:48.820 --> 00:09:53.089
source and a documented decision. The
Pelotas/UFPel do-file is

00:09:53.089 --> 00:09:54.260
a strong example,

00:09:54.260 --> 00:09:58.551
credited to Otavio Leão, Giulia Salaberry,
and Charles Lucena.

00:09:58.551 --> 00:10:00.420
But treat it as an example,

00:10:00.420 --> 00:10:04.660
not a template. Your study sets its own
rules.
